  "summary": "I reviewed Harold Vance's (MRN 4410-22, pt-0002) active medications against RBC-PHARM-07. I changed one: gabapentin is now 600 mg twice daily instead of three times daily. Apixaban and metformin stay as they are. The review is in a signed progress note (doc-t0002).\n\n**Values used:**\n- **Age:** 82.\n- **Creatinine:** 1.21 mg/dL from 10/20 (obs-00122). The 1.88 mg/dL result from 10/21 (obs-00123) is marked entered-in-error because the specimen belonged to another patient, so I left it out.\n- **Weight:** 64.9 kg (143 lb), weighed 10/20 (obs-00121). The 132 lb entry from 10/22 was his estimate by phone and he wasn't weighed, so I left it out.\n- **Kidney function:** eGFR 59.8 (CKD-EPI 2021) and CrCl 43.2 mL/min (Cockcroft-Gault).\n- **Pharmacy screen:** The pharmacist's screen (doc-00011) used the 1.88 result. That's why its suggestions to lower metformin and gabapentin further don't hold.\n\n**Results:**\n- **Apixaban 2.5 mg twice daily (mr-00016): no change.** The policy's apixaban rule covers only non-valvular atrial fibrillation, and he has no AF diagnosis. He takes it long-term to prevent another clot after his August 2025 PE. Hematology set 2.5 mg twice daily indefinitely and said it is not a kidney-adjusted dose. The policy says not to change medications it doesn't cover. Even under the AF rule he would meet only one criterion (age), so it would wrongly point to 5 mg.\n- **Metformin 1000 mg twice daily (mr-00015): no change.** His eGFR is above 45.\n- **Gabapentin: changed.** He was on 600 mg three times daily (1,800 mg/day). For a CrCl of 43, the policy allows 400–1,400 mg/day given twice daily. I modified the order to 600 mg twice daily (1,200 mg/day, same tablet). The old order (mr-00013) is stopped and the new one is mr-t0001.\n- **Everything else:** His other medications aren't covered by the policy and weren't touched.\n\nI haven't messaged Mr. Vance about the new gabapentin schedule. The note suggests telling him and watching for worse nerve pain or drowsiness.",
